# rocm-golden guest

Baked by qemu-minimal's `ansible/playbooks/vm-rocm-golden.yml`. This image
carries the drivers and userspace for three kinds of emulated device:

- an AMD Pensando ionic RDMA NIC, served by rocm-ernic
- a gfx1250 GPU, served by rocjitsu
- an SDXI engine, served by rocm-sdxi

Which of them this VM has, and how many of each, is decided by whoever
started it, not by the image. Any of the three may be absent, and there may
be several of one kind. Each section below applies only if the VM has that
device.

    kernel        {{ ansible_kernel }}  (mainline, pinned to {{ ionic_kernel_pin }})
    amdgpu-dkms   {{ golden_pkg['amdgpu-dkms'] | default('unknown') }}  (module {{ golden_amdgpu_src.stdout | trim }})
    ROCm          {{ golden_pkg['amdrocm-runtime-dev' ~ rocm_line_pin] | default('unknown') }} from the therock stream, line {{ rocm_line_pin }}
    rdma-core     {{ golden_pkg['rdma-core'] | default('unknown') }} from the archive
    rocm-ernic    {{ ernic_source_pin }}
    rocm-sdxi     {{ sdxi_source_pin }}

`/etc/rocm-golden-guest.json` has the same facts, machine-readable.

## What this VM has

The devices are attached from outside the guest, by vfio-user servers on the
host. To see what this VM was given:

    amdgpu-probe --status                  # absent: no emulated GPU
    lspci -nn | grep -E '1002:|1dd8:1002'  # GPUs and NICs on the bus
    ibv_devinfo -l                         # RDMA devices
    ls /dev/sdxi* 2>/dev/null              # SDXI engines

A VM with none of a kind is a normal configuration, not a fault: no
`/dev/kfd` node for a GPU, `ibv_devinfo` reporting no devices, and no SDXI
device node are the correct answers there.

## If the VM has emulated GPUs

`amdgpu` is blacklisted, in `/etc/modprobe.d/` and again on the kernel
command line, so a GPU that is present still needs these steps. One probe
binds every GPU on the bus.

1. Stage `ip_discovery.bin` into `/lib/firmware/amdgpu/`. Generate it with
   `rj-ip-discovery gfx1250` from the **same rocjitsu build** that serves the
   socket. It is the one firmware file this image does not carry, for exactly
   that reason. From a qemu-minimal checkout,
   `ansible-playbook vm-rocm-golden.yml --tags firmware` does this step.
   Everything else, including the generated gap set, is already in
   `{{ golden_firmware_dir }}`; do not overwrite it.
2. Load the driver:

       sudo amdgpu-probe

   It wraps `modprobe amdgpu` with the emulation parameters. `amdgpu-probe
   --status` prints `absent`, `ready`, `loaded` or `stranded` and changes
   nothing.
3. Check: a bound device under `/sys/bus/pci/drivers/amdgpu/`, a named node
   under `/sys/class/kfd/kfd/topology/nodes/`, and no failure in `sudo dmesg`.

## If the VM has rocm-ernic NICs

This image replaces the in-tree `ionic` and `ionic_rdma` with an SRQ-capable
build: rocm-ernic's `patches/srq` and `patches/p4` on the matching mainline
tag, installed as the `ionic-ernic` DKMS package, with rocm-ernic's
`patches/rdma-core` provider in `/usr/local` and the `ernic_p4` module
alongside. `/etc/ionic-srq-p4.json` records the pins.

Without SRQ, `max_srq` is 0 and UCX declines `rc_verbs`, which leaves
`ud_verbs` and no one-sided RDMA, with nothing in the failure naming SRQ.

If you rebuild `ionic-ernic` here, pass `--patches-dir` pointing at
rocm-ernic's `patches/srq`. A stock rebuild installs a driver with no SRQ
under the same package name.

To see what is loaded use `nm`, not `strings`:
`nm $(modinfo -n ionic_rdma) | grep ionic_create_srq`.

The Ethernet sides come up as `rocm-ernic0`, `rocm-ernic1` and so on, and
`rocm-ernic-netcfg.service` addresses each at boot from its own MAC. Resolve
the IB device names from `ibv_devinfo -l` instead of assuming them.

## If the VM has SDXI engines

`sdxi_char` loads at boot and `sdxi-cli` is on PATH. `sdxi_perf` is
installed and blacklisted; load it by hand when you want it.

## Things that will waste your time

- **A VM with a rocm-ernic NIC needs at least 4 vCPUs.** `ionic_rdma` fails
  inside the driver below that, not anywhere obvious.
- **A warm reboot with an emulated GPU attached does not work.**
  Cold-boot instead.
- **`modprobe` on an already-loaded amdgpu discards its parameters** and
  returns 0. `amdgpu-probe` refuses in that case.
- **A bare `modprobe amdgpu` costs a reboot.** Without the emulation
  parameters it binds nothing, and `rmmod amdgpu` has been seen to fault.
- **Without `ip_discovery.bin` amdgpu cannot probe a GPU.** The failure is
  `can't load firmware "amdgpu/ip_discovery.bin"` followed by
  `probe with driver amdgpu failed with error -2`.
- **Locked memory is unlimited here on purpose.** Past the 8192 KB default
  every `ibv_reg_mr()` fails with "Cannot allocate memory", reported a long
  way from the cause.
- **One amdgpu patch in this image is unreviewed.** `kfd_ptrace_gate` changes
  a KFD ptrace check so the driver builds on 7.2. The stamp records
  `"kfd_ptrace_gate_reviewed": false`. Do not put anything here that you
  would mind a local user reading.

## ROCm

`ROCM_PATH` is `{{ golden_rocm_path }}` and its `bin` is on PATH for login
and non-login shells alike, so `ssh guest hipcc ...` works. `/opt/rocm/bin`
and `/opt/rocm/lib` are symlinks into it. `amd-smi` is installed and
`import amdsmi` works; `rocm-smi` is deprecated upstream and not packaged in
this stream.
